discussion.body:
The current implementation is correct, but I do not think the row-oriented path is the right final execution model for aligned data.

For aligned series, all columns already share the same ordered time axis. The existing time-filter path also provides the necessary scan-pruning foundation: device/chunk/page statistics are used to determine what actually needs to be decoded. We should extend the same idea to FIELD filtering:

1. apply the time filter and prune device/chunk/pages;
2. collect all columns referenced by the FIELD filter;
3. decode those columns as aligned batches;
4. evaluate column predicates into per-column masks;
5. combine the masks according to the logical expression into one final selection mask;
6. materialize only the rows/columns required by the result and apply OFFSET/LIMIT.

This avoids per-row timestamp merging and `TableFieldRow` construction. Non-aligned series can continue to use the existing row-based path.

This batch-oriented design is also intended to address the performance regressions found in the current validation: it preserves aligned batch decoding and avoids the overhead of the per-row merge and filter path.

I plan to implement this as a follow-up rather than continuing to optimize the current per-row loop. Keeping this PR focused on correctness also makes the change easier to review.
